# tests/tcyr/namespace.tcyr — a consumer's own globals cannot reach inside chitra (cc-13, 1.1.2).
#
# ⛔ THROUGH 1.1.1 THEY COULD, AND THE RESULT WAS A CRASH, NOT A NAME CLASH. dist/chitra.cyr is a
# strip-concatenation, so every top-level `var` in src/ is a global in the CONSUMER'S program, and
# 128 of them carried no chitra prefix (RAW_*, CUR_*, IMAGE_*_OFFSET, HT_*, BR_*, JF_*, BH_*, LZ_*,
# the IDCT FIX_* constants, the GIF/BMP header constants, _jpeg_zz_*). A consumer that declared its
# own global of one of those names AFTER linking chitra — or merely assigned one — rewrote the value
# chitra decodes with. Measured on 1.1.1 with the value 0: `CUR_POS_OFFSET` made the JPEG and PNG
# decoders SIGSEGV, `LZ_BIT` made the GIF decoder SIGSEGV, and `HT_SIZE` broke every JPEG. A
# declaration got only a "duplicate symbol" warning; an assignment got nothing at all.
# 1.1.2 renamed every one of them `_CHITRA_*` / `_chitra_*`, and scripts/check-surface.sh now fails
# `make lint` on any unprefixed top-level `var` in the bundle, so the class cannot come back.
#
# ⛔ FUNCTIONS TOO (1.1.3). 84 internal helpers (`_jpeg_*`, `_bmp_*`, `_cur_*`, `_gif_*`, `_chunk_*`,
# `_adam7_*`, `_png_*`) had no prefix either, and a consumer function of the same name REPLACED
# chitra's — "duplicate fn … last definition wins", a warning and nothing more. Measured on 1.1.2: a
# consumer `_jpeg_descale` made a JPEG decode to different pixels. 1.1.3 renamed them `_chitra_*`,
# and check-surface.sh now fails on any unprefixed top-level `fn` as well.
#
# ⚠ THIS SUITE IS THE CONSUMER. It declares the pre-1.1.2 names AFTER `src/lib.cyr`, each set to
# 3 (a misaligned offset, a nonsense size, a corrupt IDCT constant), then decodes one small file per
# format and checks the pixels against the reference decoders. Against the 1.1.1 sources it fails;
# with the rename it passes, and the consumer's globals still read 3 afterwards — chitra neither
# reads nor writes them.
# 127 of the 128 are here. `_jpeg_zz_tbl` is not: it was an ARRAY, and redeclaring it as a scalar is
# a hard compile error ("redeclared with a different type"), which is loud and was never the hazard.
# Fixtures (7x5, one per format) were made with ImageMagick and cjpeg; the expected sums are
# ImageMagick's for PNG / BMP / GIF and `djpeg -nosmooth`'s for the JPEG.
include "src/lib.cyr"
include "lib/assert.cyr"

